# FluxRadar PostgreSQL backups (single Hetzner Docker host).
#
# Install as root, once, and keep it out of the release directory so a deploy can
# never change the schedule silently:
#
#   install -m 0700 -o fluxradar -g fluxradar -d /opt/fluxradar/backups
#   install -m 0644 -o root -g root \
#     /opt/fluxradar/current/deploy/backup/fluxradar-backup.cron \
#     /etc/cron.d/fluxradar-backup
#
# The first line matters: cron opens the log file below BEFORE the script runs,
# so a missing directory would make the job fail without ever starting.
#
# Replace "fluxradar" below with the user that owns /opt/fluxradar and is in the
# docker group — the same account the deploy connects as. The scripts read the
# encryption key and the S3 credentials from the release env file (mode 600), so
# no secret appears in this file or in cron mail.
#
# Both jobs log to /opt/fluxradar/backups/backup.log — a path the deploy user
# owns. /var/log would need root, and a cron job that cannot open its own log
# file fails silently, which is exactly the failure this file exists to remove.
# Keep MAILTO pointed at an address a human reads for the same reason, and add a
# logrotate entry for that file once it has been running for a while.

SHELL=/bin/bash
PATH=/usr/local/sbin:/usr/local/bin:/usr/sbin:/usr/bin:/sbin:/bin
MAILTO=root

# Daily snapshot at 02:17 UTC — off the hour so it does not collide with every
# other cron job on the machine.
17 2 * * * fluxradar /opt/fluxradar/current/deploy/backup/pg-backup.sh >> /opt/fluxradar/backups/backup.log 2>&1

# Weekly proof that the newest snapshot actually restores. It restores into a
# throwaway database beside the live one and drops it again; the live database is
# never touched. A failure here means the backups are not backups.
30 3 * * 0 fluxradar /opt/fluxradar/current/deploy/backup/pg-restore.sh --verify-latest >> /opt/fluxradar/backups/backup.log 2>&1
